Objective
Learn the stages of the Cyber Kill Chain framework and understand how attackers conduct cyberattacks from initial reconnaissance to achieving their objectives. The room also demonstrates how defenders can detect and disrupt attacks at different phases of the intrusion lifecycle.

Step 1: Introduction to the Cyber Kill Chain
The Cyber Kill Chain is a cybersecurity framework developed by Lockheed Martin to model the stages of a cyberattack.
The framework helps security teams:


Understand attacker behavior


Detect threats earlier


Improve defensive strategies


Interrupt attacks before compromise occurs


The Cyber Kill Chain consists of seven stages:


Reconnaissance


Weaponization


Delivery


Exploitation


Installation


Command and Control (C2)


Actions on Objectives


Key takeaway:
Understanding attacker methodology helps defenders identify and stop threats before major damage occurs.

Step 2: Reconnaissance
Reconnaissance is the information-gathering phase where attackers collect intelligence about a target.
Common reconnaissance techniques:


Open-source intelligence (OSINT)


Social media research


DNS enumeration


Email harvesting


Network scanning


Tools attackers may use:


Nmap


WHOIS


Shodan


Maltego


The goal is to identify vulnerabilities, employees, exposed services, and attack opportunities.
Key takeaway:
Reconnaissance often leaves traces that defenders can monitor and detect.

Step 3: Weaponization
During weaponization, attackers prepare malicious payloads to exploit vulnerabilities.
Examples include:


Malware creation


Malicious Office documents


Ransomware payloads


Exploit kits


Trojanized software


Attackers combine:


Exploits


Payloads


Delivery mechanisms


into a weaponized attack package.
Key takeaway:
Weaponization usually occurs outside the target environment, making detection more difficult.

Step 4: Delivery
Delivery is the stage where attackers transmit malicious payloads to victims.
Common delivery methods:


Phishing emails


Malicious attachments


Drive-by downloads


USB devices


Malicious websites


Phishing remains one of the most common attack vectors in modern cyberattacks.
Defensive measures include:


Email filtering


Web filtering


User awareness training


Attachment sandboxing


Key takeaway:
Many attacks can be stopped during delivery through strong security awareness and filtering controls.

Step 5: Exploitation
Exploitation occurs when malicious code executes on the victim system.
Examples:


Exploiting software vulnerabilities


Macro execution


Privilege escalation


Browser exploits


Attackers often target:


Unpatched systems


Weak configurations


Outdated applications


Defenders reduce risk by:


Applying security patches


Disabling unnecessary services


Using Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)


Key takeaway:
Patch management and endpoint protection are critical defensive controls.

Step 6: Installation
In this stage, attackers establish persistence on compromised systems.
Common persistence methods:


Registry modifications


Scheduled tasks


Startup folder persistence


Backdoors


Malware installation


The attacker ensures they can maintain access even after system reboots.
Defenders may detect installation activity through:


File integrity monitoring


Antivirus alerts


Behavioral monitoring


Endpoint telemetry


Key takeaway:
Persistence mechanisms are strong indicators of compromise within an environment.

Step 7: Command and Control (C2)
Command and Control allows attackers to remotely communicate with infected systems.
Common C2 techniques:


HTTP/HTTPS traffic


DNS tunneling


Encrypted channels


Remote access trojans (RATs)


Attackers use C2 servers to:


Send commands


Exfiltrate data


Deploy additional malware


Move laterally


Security teams monitor:


Abnormal outbound traffic


Beaconing patterns


Suspicious domains


Network anomalies


Key takeaway:
Network monitoring plays a major role in detecting command-and-control activity.

Step 8: Actions on Objectives
This is the final phase where attackers accomplish their goals.
Potential objectives include:


Data theft


Ransomware deployment


Credential harvesting


Destruction of systems


Financial fraud


Espionage


At this point, attackers may already have significant access to the environment.
Incident response teams work to:


Contain the attack


Eradicate malware


Recover systems


Investigate impact


Key takeaway:
The earlier defenders interrupt the kill chain, the less damage attackers can cause.

Defensive Security Perspective
One of the most important lessons from the room is that defenders do not need to stop attackers at every stage.
Interrupting the attack at any point in the kill chain can successfully prevent compromise.
Examples:
